From 78ff72e7b0dd868411c428968d2464eddb7d93ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noktoborus <noktoborus@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:50:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] Input: axp20x-pek: short and long press, and the power
 off time, from the device tree

The AXP22x tells a short press and a long press of its key apart by
itself (IRQ18, IRQ19), and keeps the time of the long press and of the
hardware power off in REG 36H (AXP228 datasheet, 9.1, REG 36H, REG 42H).
The driver sent KEY_POWER on the edges of the key only, and the times
could be set from sysfs only.

Read from the node of the PMIC:

- pek-short-press, pek-long-press: child nodes with linux,input-type
  (EV_KEY, the default, or EV_SW) and linux,code, as gpio-keys has them.
  A key goes down and up on its press; a switch turns over. With either
  node, the key sends their events only, not KEY_POWER on its edges.
  pek-long-press may have x-powers,time-ms: 1000, 1500, 2000 or 2500.
- x-powers,pek-power-off-ms: the time of the hardware power off, 4000,
  6000, 8000 or 10000; 0 turns it off.

With none of them, the key works as before. Only the AXP22x (axp221-pek)
reads them: the registers were checked in its datasheet only; on another
PMIC a warning says they are left out. A node that is wrong is left out
with a warning.

A use: a short press as a lid (EV_SW, SW_LID), a long press as the
power key (EV_KEY, KEY_POWER).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
